so this program is very very ambitious  and that it's trying to answer one of  the big questions of our time what is  intelligence and how learning can  contribute to it it starts from the  hypothesis that there may be a few  simple principles like the laws of  physics which could explain human  intelligence animal intelligence but  also allow us to build intelligent  machines and so we're trying to sort of  figure out some new techniques that  would allow machines to learn very large  amount of background knowledge about the  world about how the world works by  observation a little bit like like  babies and young animals and that will  take AI to the next step so this  research is has very long term goals but  is already having incredible impact in  society today so when you hear the terms  a I and D planning it's in large part  due to the progress we've made in this  group so that's one of the things that  we want to develop in the NMD program  you know figure out if the brain uses  any kind of learning mechanisms that are  similar to the ones that we think are  for machines and vice versa of course  get inspiration from what we learn about  other brain works for more machines so  see far has been an incredible social  environment for the group of people  behind this program so we bring people  you know in small workshops we bring  students and in summer schools and it  seems like this to kind of build a  vocabulary a way to think about things  to exchange ideas very early before you  know you would publish a paper so CFR  has had a crucial role in fostering  those interactions that accelerate  progress  [Music]  you
 

